# Line endings are a property of the repository, not of whoever cloned it.
#
# Windows batch files must use CRLF: cmd.exe mis-parses LF-only batch files,
# particularly around labels and `goto`, and the failure is silent until someone
# runs the script. Before this file existed the correct endings came only from
# the developer's local `core.autocrlf=true`; a clone on Linux (or with
# autocrlf=false) produced LF files that would not run on the factory server.
#
# tests/test_system.py::test_windows_launch_scripts_use_cmd_safe_encoding asserts
# both properties (UTF-8 BOM and CRLF) for start.bat, install.bat, backup.bat and
# settings.example.bat, so this is enforced rather than merely documented.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Shell scripts are the opposite: CRLF makes them unrunnable on Linux.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Binary assets must never be normalised.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.gz binary

# The built front end is byte-exact output, not source.
#
# Its compressed copies are committed beside it (tools/build_frontend.py writes
# them, traceability/responses.py serves them), and they are generated from the
# bytes the build produced. Normalising line endings on checkout rewrites the .js
# and .css but not the .gz, because git treats the .gz as binary — so a clone
# with core.autocrlf=true ends up with a .js on disk that is not what the .gz
# contains, and the server sends the committed bytes while the file next to it
# says something else.
#
# That is not hypothetical: it failed CI on the Windows runner while passing on
# Linux, where no conversion happens. tests/test_static_compression.py decompresses
# what the server sent and compares it to the file, which is what caught it.
static/dist/** -text
